   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the general business law and the tax law, in relation to\nenacting the \"New York State Transparency in Supply Chains Act\", and to\nrequire businesses within the state to disclose their preventative measures\nagainst human trafficking",
    "basePrintNo": "S748",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S748-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add §399-mm, Gen Bus L; add §220, Tax L",
    "lawSection": "General Business Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S748\n\nSPONSOR: BIAGGI\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the general business law and the tax law, in relation to\nenacting the \"New York State Transparency in Supply Chains Act\", and to\nrequire businesses within the state to disclose their preventative meas-\nures against human trafficking\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThis bill will work to eradicate slavery and human trafficking within\nsupply chains worldwide by providing transparency within the supply\nchains of large corporations doing business within the state of New\nYork.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 establishes the title of the bill.\n\nSection 2 sets forth legislative findings\n\nSection 3 amends the general business law by adding a new section that\ndefines what business is to be held accountable under this law, and\nwhich revenue streams are exempt from total revenue used to calculate\ngross recipients, including but not limited to repayment and maturity on\na loan, damages received as a result of litigation, and tax refunds.\nThis section also defines manufacturer and retail sellers as they would\nbe implicated in this law and what amount of gross recipients would\nqualify said manufacturer or retail seller.  Additionally, this section\nsets out the provisions of how the information will be made available,\nand necessary information to disclose, such as if the auditor was a\nthird party. Audits must investigate how thoroughly the retail seller or\nmanufacturer verifies the product supply chains to address risks of\ntrafficking, address audits of suppliers to evaluate supplier compliance\nwith company standards.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe bill recognizes that human trafficking is a near-universal issue\nthat globally generates $100 million in revenue annually. Within the\nnext 10 years, experts expect that human trafficking will surpass both\ndrug and arms trafficking. Victims of these crimes are disproportionate-\nly poor women and people of color, with one-fourth of trafficking\nvictims being children. This issue is incredibly difficult to track and\neradicate, with the market acting as a large inhibitor to these efforts\nbecause of opaque supply chains. Large businesses that provide goods\nsourced with materials from the globalized production economy have the\nmoral obligation to use their resources to ensure that unfree labor has\nno place in their supply chains. By requiring those businesses to\nconduct an audit of human trafficking within their supply chains and\nensure that information is available to the public, greater accountabil-\nity will ensue, moving closer to attaining the goal of supply chains\nfree and clear of forced labor.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n\n2020: S6282/A9537 - Died in Consumer Protection/Died in Economic Devel-\nopment\n\n \nFISCAL IMPACT ON THE STATE:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
